:: start_2
There’s a moment in a relationship when everything shifts quietly no fireworks, no dramatic reveal just a soft realization that this person has woven themselves into your life without trying. With Ava, that moment came somewhere around half year...

We had fallen into an easy rhythm, shared café mornings, small walks between classes, evenings curled up watching movies. She wasn’t loud about her affection, she showed it in tiny gestures. A thumb brushing my palm. Her head leaning onto my shoulder. The way she’d whisper my name when she thought I wasn’t listening.

One autumn afternoon, we sat on a bench near the literature building, leaves scattering around us. She wasn’t reading, she was watching the wind move through the trees. Her voice was barely there when she said,

<<ava "This is my favorite kind of day.">>

<<leo "Why?">>

<<ava "Because everything slows down enough to breathe.">>

That line stayed with me. Ava saw the world softly, and somehow she made me want to see it that way too. Around that time, our closeness deepened. Kissing her became a language slow, warm, shy but full of something growing. 

She’d melt into me one moment, then pause the next, breath trembling, fingers curling at my shirt. Her boundaries were gentle but firm. When things heated too quickly, she’d whisper,

<<ava "Not yet…">>

Though the wanting inside me tightened, I always answered,

<<leo "Whenever you’re ready.">>

Because loving her wasn’t just wanting her body it was wanting her trust and she gave it in all these small, careful ways.

Over time, we built a life together in small rituals. Saturday café visits. Evening walks. Movie nights with her tucked against me, tracing tiny circles on my ribs when she got sleepy.

She wasn’t afraid of me she was afraid of rushing herself. Afraid of stepping over a line she wasn’t ready to approach yet. But she always came back to me, always curled into my arms afterward, trusting me with both her desire and her hesitation.

By One year, I knew I loved her deeply. Not suddenly, but through a thousand quiet moments. The way she bit her lip when she was nervous. The way she looked at me before every kiss, checking that I wanted her too. The way her boundaries weren’t walls they were steps she was building slowly.

I learned patience from her. Not the strained kind, but patience born of love. The kind that makes waiting feel like part of the journey. By the time a year passed, I wasn’t wondering whether she was the right person. I was wondering how I’d gotten so lucky to be the one she trusted.

